<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<exercise xml:id="problem-continuous-pointwise-limits-of-discontinuous-maps" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>
    Continuous pointwise limits of discontinuous maps
  </title>
  
  <introduction>
    <p>
      We have seen in <xref ref="theorem-uniform-limits-of-continuous-maps"/> and <xref ref="problem-uniform-limits-of-uniformly-continuous-maps"/> that uniform limits preserve continuity and uniform continuity. In this problem, we will construct counterexamples to the converses of these results.
    </p>
  </introduction>
  
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Give an example of a sequence <m> \of{ f_{ n } }_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty } </m> of continuous maps <m> f_{ n } \colon X \to Y </m> from a metric space <m> X </m> to a metric space <m> Y </m> which converges pointwise but not uniformly to a continuous map <m> f \colon X \to Y </m> from <m> X </m> to <m> Y </m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    
    <!-- <answer>
      <p>
        For each index <m> n \in \N </m>, let <m> f_{ n } \colon \R \to \R </m> be the real-valued function defined by the formula
        
        <md>
          f_{ n } \of{ x } = \frac{ x^{ 2 } }{ 2^{ n } }
        </md>.
        
        Moreover, let <m> f \colon \R \to \R </m> be the constant real-valued function defined by the formula <m> f \of{ x } = 0 </m>.
      </p>
    </answer> -->
  </task>
  
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Give an example of a sequence <m> \of{ g_{ n } }_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty } </m> of discontinuous maps <m> g_{ n } \colon X \to Y </m> from a metric space <m> X </m> to a metric space <m> Y </m> which converges uniformly to a continuous map <m> g \colon X \to Y </m> from <m> X </m> to <m> Y </m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    
    <!-- <answer>
      <p>
        For each index <m> n \in \N </m>, let <m> g_{ n } \colon \R \to \R </m> be the real-valued function defined piecewise by the formula
        
        <md>
          f_{ n } \of{ x } =
            \begin{cases}
              \frac{ 1 }{ 2^{ n } } \amp x = 0 \\
              0 \amp x \neq 0
            \end{cases}
        </md>.
        
        Moreover, let <m> g \colon \R \to \R </m> be the constant real-valued function defined by the formula <m> f \of{ x } = 0 </m>.
      </p>
    </answer> -->
  </task>
</exercise>
